--TEST--
Core\Arr::prepend renumbers every key and ::withoutFirst renumbers none, where array_unshift and array_shift each renumber the integers and keep the strings
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
`rule:types/preserve-keys` refuses any rule that asks what type a key is, and PHP's two front-end functions are both such a rule: `array_unshift` and `array_shift` renumber the integer keys of what remains and leave the string ones untouched, so the same call answers a list or a map depending on the subject. Novis answers uniformly at both ends and the two uniform answers point opposite ways, because the two operations lose different things: prepending has no non-arbitrary key to give a new front entry, so `Core\Arr::prepend` renumbers everything and always returns a list, while dropping an entry takes nothing away from the survivors, so `Core\Arr::withoutFirst` keeps every key and a caller who wanted `0, 1, …` writes `Core\Arr::values` and says so. Keeping is the direction that loses nothing: a map's keys cannot be recovered once a member has discarded them.
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Render {
    public static function of(array<int> $a): string {
        var $out = "";
        foreach ($a as string $k => int $v) {
            $out = $out . $k . "=" . $v . ";";
        }
        return $out;
    }
}

array<int> $list = [10, 20, 30];
array<int> $map = ["x" => 1, "y" => 2];
array<int> $mixed = ["5" => 1, "x" => 2, "9" => 3];

// `array_unshift($map, 7, 8)` answers `0=7;1=8;x=1;y=2;`, a map whose two new
// entries are integer-keyed; over `$mixed` it answers `0=7;1=8;2=1;x=2;3=3;`,
// where the surviving keys now depend on how many *earlier* entries happened
// to be integer-keyed. Novis's answer is a list in both cases.
echo Render::of(Core\Arr::prepend($map, 7, 8)), "\n";
echo Render::of(Core\Arr::prepend($mixed, 7, 8)), "\n";

// The opposite direction, and the row that shows it is not simply "Novis
// renumbers more": over a plain list `array_shift` answers `0=20;1=30;` while
// `withoutFirst` keeps the keys the survivors already had. This is the only
// member of the family that diverges on a list.
echo Render::of(Core\Arr::withoutFirst($list)), "\n";

// The agreeing row: with no integer key to renumber, PHP's rule has nothing to
// do and both languages keep `y`. The divergence is entirely in what happens
// to an integer key, on both members.
echo Render::of(Core\Arr::withoutFirst($map)), "\n";

// Both kinds of key at once, where PHP answers `x=2;0=3;` — one survivor kept
// its key and the other did not.
echo Render::of(Core\Arr::withoutFirst($mixed)), "\n";
--EXPECT--
0=7;1=8;2=1;3=2;
0=7;1=8;2=1;3=2;4=3;
1=20;2=30;
y=2;
x=2;9=3;
